</head>
<body>
    <div class="section">
        <div class="container">
            <p class="install-laravel">Understanding Laravel Middleware with Real-World Examples</p>

            <div class="author-info">
                <p class="umer-shahzad">Muhammad Umer Shahzad</p>
                <p class="info"><i class="fas fa-calendar-alt"></i><em>Published on: <span id="publish-date">September 27, 2024</span></em></p>
                <p class="info"><i class="fas fa-clock"></i><em>Estimated reading time: <span id="reading-time">7 minutes</span></em></p>
            </div>

            <h2>What is Middleware?</h2>
            <p>Laravel middleware acts as a filter for HTTP requests entering your application. It's a powerful concept that helps you add functionality around your routes — like authentication, logging, or CORS handling — without bloating your controller logic.</p>
            <p>Think of middleware as a gatekeeper. Before a request hits your controller, Laravel checks if any middleware needs to act on it. For example, a middleware could check if a user is authenticated before allowing them to view a page.</p>

            <h2>Creating Middleware</h2>
            <p>To create a new middleware, run the following Artisan command:</p>
            
            <div class="code-container">
                <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyToClipboard('#code1')"><i class="fas fa-copy"></i> Copy</button>
                <code id="code1">php artisan make:middleware CheckRole</code>
            </div>
            
            <p>This command creates a new middleware file in <code>app/Http/Middleware</code>.</p>

            <h3>Sample Middleware Logic:</h3>
            <div class="code-container">
                <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyToClipboard('#code2')"><i class="fas fa-copy"></i> Copy</button>
                <code id="code2">public function handle($request, Closure $next)
{
    if (auth()->check() && auth()->user()->role == 'admin') {
        return $next($request);
    }

    return redirect('/login');
}</code>
            </div>

            <h2>Registering Middleware</h2>
            <p>Add your middleware to the <code>$routeMiddleware</code> array in <code>app/Http/Kernel.php</code>:</p>
            
            <div class="code-container">
                <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyToClipboard('#code3')"><i class="fas fa-copy"></i> Copy</button>
                <code id="code3">'check.role' => \App\Http\Middleware\CheckRole::class,</code>
            </div>

            <h2>Using Middleware in Routes</h2>
            <p>You can apply middleware to routes in several ways. Here's the most common approach:</p>
            
            <div class="code-container">
                <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyToClipboard('#code4')"><i class="fas fa-copy"></i> Copy</button>
                <code id="code4">Route::get('/admin', function () {
    return view('admin.dashboard');
})->middleware('check.role');</code>
            </div>

            <h2>Middleware Groups</h2>
            <p>Laravel allows you to group middleware for easier application to routes:</p>
            
            <div class="code-container">
                <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyToClipboard('#code5')"><i class="fas fa-copy"></i> Copy</button>
                <code id="code5">Route::middleware(['auth', 'verified'])->group(function () {
    Route::get('/dashboard', function () {
        return view('dashboard');
    });
});</code>
            </div>

            <h2>Real-World Use Cases</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Authentication:</strong> Verify user is logged in before accessing protected routes</li>
                <li><strong>Authorization:</strong> Check user roles or permissions</li>
                <li><strong>Logging:</strong> Record user activity or API requests</li>
                <li><strong>Localization:</strong> Set language preferences based on user settings</li>
                <li><strong>API Throttling:</strong> Limit request rates to prevent abuse</li>
                <li><strong>CORS Handling:</strong> Manage cross-origin resource sharing policies</li>
                <li><strong>Maintenance Mode:</strong> Temporarily disable access during updates</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Middleware Parameters</h2>
            <p>You can pass parameters to middleware for more flexible behavior:</p>
            
            <div class="code-container">
                <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyToClipboard('#code6')"><i class="fas fa-copy"></i> Copy</button>
                <code id="code6">Route::get('/posts/{post}', function (Post $post) {
    return view('post.show', ['post' => $post]);
})->middleware('can:view,post');</code>
            </div>

            <h2>Terminable Middleware</h2>
            <p>Some middleware needs to do work after the response is sent to the browser. You can create terminable middleware by adding a <code>terminate</code> method:</p>
            
            <div class="code-container">
                <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyToClipboard('#code7')"><i class="fas fa-copy"></i> Copy</button>
                <code id="code7">public function terminate($request, $response)
{
    // Log the request after the response is sent
    Log::info('Request completed', [
        'url' => $request->url(),
        'status' => $response->status()
    ]);
}</code>
            </div>

            <h2>Conclusion</h2>
            <p>Middleware helps you write cleaner, more maintainable Laravel applications by separating concerns and enhancing the security and flexibility of your app. With the examples provided, you should now have a solid foundation for implementing middleware in your own projects.</p>
            <p>Remember that middleware is just one tool in Laravel's powerful feature set. Use it judiciously to keep your application organized and secure.</p>

            <div class="comment-section">
                <h3>Comments</h3>
                <textarea class="comment-box" id="comment-box" placeholder="Add your comment here..."></textarea>
                <button class="submit-comment" id="submit-comment">Submit Comment</button>
                <div class="comments-list" id="comments-list"></div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <script>
        // Simulate blog post ID for AJAX (replace with your dynamic ID logic)
        const postId = '12345';

        // Function to handle comment submission
        $(document).on('click', '#submit-comment', function () {
            const commentText = $('#comment-box').val().trim();
            if (!commentText) {
                alert('Please write a comment before submitting.');
                return;
            }

            // AJAX POST request to submit the comment
            $.ajax({
                url: '/submit-comment', // Replace with your backend endpoint
                method: 'POST',
                contentType: 'application/json',
                data: JSON.stringify({
                    postId: postId,
                    comment: commentText
                }),
                success: function (response) {
                    // Append the new comment to the comment list
                    $('#comments-list').prepend(`
                        <div class="comment">
                            <p class="author">You</p>
                            <p class="timestamp">Just now</p>
                            <p class="text">${commentText}</p>
                        </div>
                    `);

                    // Clear the comment box
                    $('#comment-box').val('');
                },
                error: function () {
                    alert('Failed to post comment. Please try again later.');
                }
            });
        });

        // Function to load comments via AJAX
        function loadComments() {
            $.ajax({
                url: `/get-comments?postId=${postId}`, // Replace with your backend endpoint
                method: 'GET',
                success: function (comments) {
                    comments.forEach(comment => {
                        $('#comments-list').append(`
                            <div class="comment">
                                <p class="author">${comment.author}</p>
                                <p class="timestamp">${comment.timestamp}</p>
                                <p class="text">${comment.text}</p>
                            </div>
                        `);
                    });
                },
                error: function () {
                    $('#comments-list').html('<p>Failed to load comments. Please try again later.</p>');
                }
            });
        }

        // Load comments on page load
        $(document).ready(function () {
            loadComments();
        });

        function copyToClipboard(elementId) {
            const copyText = document.querySelector(elementId);
            const textArea = document.createElement("textarea");
            textArea.value = copyText.innerText;
            document.body.appendChild(textArea);
            textArea.select();
            document.execCommand("copy");
            document.body.removeChild(textArea);

            const message = document.createElement("div");
            message.innerText = "Copied Successfully!";
            message.style.position = "fixed";
            message.style.bottom = "20px";
            message.style.right = "20px";
            message.style.backgroundColor = "#28a745";
            message.style.color = "white";
            message.style.padding = "10px 20px";
            message.style.borderRadius = "5px";
            message.style.zIndex = "9999";
            message.style.fontSize = "14px";
            document.body.appendChild(message);
            setTimeout(() => {
                message.remove();
            }, 2000);
        }

        function calculateReadingTime() {
            const blogContent = document.body.innerText;

            const wordCount = blogContent.split(/\s+/).filter(word => word.length > 0).length;

            const wordsPerMinute = 200;
            const readingTime = Math.ceil(wordCount / wordsPerMinute);

            const readingTimeElement = document.getElementById('reading-time');
            readingTimeElement.innerText = `${readingTime} minute${readingTime > 1 ? 's' : ''}`;
        }

        document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", calculateReadingTime);
    </script>
</body>
</html>
